        "call": "Fetch BEA's May, June, July, and August 2026 Personal Income and Outlays release pages listed in sourceContext.",
        "result": "Own-month first-print core PCE monthly changes were May 0.3%, June 0.1%, July 0.2%, and August 0.2%. August real PCE increased 0.6%. July's first print of 0.2% differs from the revised 0.1% shown in August's release; the historical sample retains July's first print."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The outside-view reference class is the eight fetched January-August 2026 first prints: [0.4, 0.4, 0.3, 0.2, 0.3, 0.1, 0.2, 0.2]%. Their base rate mean is 2.1/8 = 0.2625%, while the June-August mean is 0.5/3 = 0.166667%. Recent momentum therefore supports an estimate below the full-sample mean."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "web.run",
        "call": "Fetch https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/cpi_09112026.htm.",
        "result": "August seasonally adjusted core CPI increased 0.3%, versus July's 0.2%; services excluding energy increased 0.3% and core goods 0.1%. Airline fares increased 2.7% and lodging away from home 2.4%, while medical care declined 0.2% and vehicle insurance 0.8%. September CPI is scheduled for October 14, 2026."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "web.run",
        "call": "Fetch https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/ppi_09102026.htm.",
        "result": "August seasonally adjusted final-demand PPI increased 0.4%; goods excluding food and energy increased 0.4%, services 0.1%, and final demand excluding food, energy, and trade services 0.3%. September PPI is scheduled for October 15, 2026."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Level: August core PCE's annual inflation supplies background rather than a direct monthly forecast. Momentum: recent core PCE prints warrant a downward adjustment from the historical mean. One-off effects: August travel rebounds may reverse, so their full increases are not extrapolated. Policy mechanism: no separate immediate policy adjustment is imposed. CPI and PPI are distinct proxy series, not target-history substitutes; their August readings support only a small judgmental upward adjustment. September readings were unavailable at run time."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: Use an equal-weight blend of the eight-print January-August first-print mean and the June-August persistence mean. Starting prior 0.2625%, momentum adjustment = (0.166667 - 0.2625)/2 = -0.047917 percentage points. Add a judgmental 0.02 percentage points for August services and core goods pressure; additional one-off and policy adjustments are zero. The implied estimate is 0.234583%, rounded to 0.23%. For this change series, calculate dispersion from the observed values themselves: sum squared deviations = 0.07875; sigma = 0.106066 percentage points = sqrt(0.07875/7). A normal approximation to the 80% forecast interval uses half-width 1.28*sigma = 0.135765. No further widening or narrowing is applied. Final bounds are 0.23 +/- 0.135765 = [0.094235, 0.365765]%, reported as [0.094, 0.366]%. This is a small-sample forecast interval, not an interval for the historical mean."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Point = round(0.5*(2.1/8) + 0.5*(0.5/3) + 0.02, 2) = 0.23. Sigma = sqrt(0.07875/(8-1)) = 0.106066. Half-width = 1.28*0.106066 = 0.135765. Rounded 80% bounds = [0.094, 0.366]."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The upside risk is continued travel increases combined with stronger medical services and core goods pricing; a rounded 0.4% print would land above the interval. The downside risk is reversal of travel rebounds alongside broader goods discounting and softer services; a 0.0% or negative print would land below the interval. These scenarios are outside the interval. Unpublished September CPI/PPI and the annual update limit confidence in persistence."
      },
